1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,400
This is Dario.

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:07,000
Five years ago, he walked out of OpenAI with six colleagues, and started a rival.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,400
On Thursday, that rival goes public.

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:14,500
The price tag? $1.4 trillion.

5
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:17,000
FRANK: Good for him. I'm not buying it.

6
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:22,500
This is Frank. Frank is 58. Frank has a retirement account.

7
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:25,600
Frank, do you own a Nasdaq-100 fund?

8
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:27,100
FRANK: …Yes.

9
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:32,500
Then within a few weeks, you may be buying it anyway. At whatever price it's trading.

10
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:33,900
FRANK: Nobody asked me.

11
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:37,700
Nobody has to. And here's what you'd be buying.

12
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:44,000
A company that just reported a loss of $80.7 billion. In six months.

13
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:45,600
FRANK: Eighty billion?!

14
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,300
And investors still ordered six times more shares than are for sale.

15
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:56,100
So either they have all lost their minds, or that loss is not what it looks like.

16
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,500
The answer decides who really wins on Thursday.

17
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:01,600
And it probably isn't Frank.

18
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:02,600
FRANK: Hey.

19
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:05,115
Start with the deal.

20
00:01:05,165 --> 00:01:11,360
Anthropic is selling 825 million shares, at $70 to $78 each.

21
00:01:11,670 --> 00:01:14,125
FRANK: 78 bucks. That's not so bad.

22
00:01:14,475 --> 00:01:17,025
Times 825 million.

23
00:01:17,575 --> 00:01:18,590
FRANK: …That's worse.

24
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:22,200
It would raise up to $64 billion.

25
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:27,580
The second largest stock market debut in history, behind SpaceX earlier this year.

26
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:33,377
Now, the valuation. You will see two numbers in the headlines, and both are real.

27
00:01:33,427 --> 00:01:39,065
Count only the shares that exist today, and Anthropic is worth about $1 trillion.

28
00:01:39,115 --> 00:01:45,890
Count every share that has been promised to employees and investors, and you get $1.4 trillion.

29
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:47,360
FRANK: So which one is it?

30
00:01:47,710 --> 00:01:48,770
Depends who's selling.

31
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:53,660
Either way, it is less than the $2 trillion that was being whispered a few months ago.

32
00:01:53,970 --> 00:01:57,552
FRANK: Only on Wall Street is $1.4 trillion a disappointment.

33
00:01:57,902 --> 00:02:00,462
Remember that number. We will come back to it.

34
00:02:01,512 --> 00:02:02,694
Now, that loss.

35
00:02:02,744 --> 00:02:09,678
In the first half of this year, Anthropic brought in $16.3 billion in revenue.

36
00:02:09,728 --> 00:02:14,197
And it reported a net loss of $80.7 billion.

37
00:02:14,507 --> 00:02:16,579
FRANK: I ran a hardware store for thirty years.

38
00:02:16,629 --> 00:02:20,292
If I lost five times what I took in, they'd have taken the keys.

39
00:02:20,642 --> 00:02:26,226
Fair. But about $72 billion of that loss is an accounting charge.

40
00:02:26,276 --> 00:02:30,561
Anthropic had borrowed money using notes that convert into shares.

41
00:02:30,611 --> 00:02:36,195
When the company became more valuable, those notes became more expensive on paper.

42
00:02:36,245 --> 00:02:38,362
No cash left the building.

43
00:02:38,672 --> 00:02:43,005
FRANK: So they lost $72 billion… by doing well.

44
00:02:43,355 --> 00:02:45,170
On paper. Yes.

45
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:47,545
FRANK: I'd like my mortgage to be on paper.

46
00:02:47,895 --> 00:02:54,779
Strip that out, and the loss from actually running the business was under $700 million.

47
00:02:54,829 --> 00:02:59,265
In its most recent reported quarter, Anthropic made an operating profit.

48
00:02:59,575 --> 00:03:00,504
FRANK: So it's safe.

49
00:03:00,854 --> 00:03:01,774
No.

50
00:03:02,084 --> 00:03:03,712
FRANK: You're doing this on purpose.

51
00:03:04,062 --> 00:03:08,594
Here is the other side. To run AI, you need computing power.

52
00:03:08,644 --> 00:03:12,793
Anthropic spent nearly $9 billion on it in six months.

53
00:03:12,843 --> 00:03:16,993
That is more than it spent in all of last year.

54
00:03:17,043 --> 00:03:20,811
And it has signed contracts to keep spending for years.

55
00:03:20,861 --> 00:03:27,302
Its filing shows a commitment to Google alone of at least $121 billion.

56
00:03:27,612 --> 00:03:29,075
FRANK: To one supplier.

57
00:03:29,425 --> 00:03:30,840
To one supplier.

58
00:03:30,890 --> 00:03:35,700
That is the bet. Revenue has to keep growing faster than the bills.

59
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:39,255
So what does this company actually sell?

60
00:03:39,565 --> 00:03:42,297
FRANK: The chatbot. My grandson uses one for his homework.

61
00:03:42,647 --> 00:03:46,671
Probably not this one. Most people have never used Claude.

62
00:03:46,721 --> 00:03:49,116
Anthropic's money comes mainly from businesses.

63
00:03:49,166 --> 00:03:58,487
Banks, software companies, and law firms pay to run Claude inside their own products, and they pay by how much they use it.

64
00:03:58,537 --> 00:04:01,142
And they are using a lot more.

65
00:04:01,192 --> 00:04:08,350
According to the filing, customers who were paying a year ago are now spending about nine times as much.

66
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:12,200
FRANK: Nine times. My best customer once bought a second ladder.

67
00:04:12,550 --> 00:04:16,621
Revenue in the second quarter was more than double the first quarter.

68
00:04:16,671 --> 00:04:19,712
Not double in a year. Double in three months.

69
00:04:19,762 --> 00:04:22,573
Either a new industry is being born.

70
00:04:22,623 --> 00:04:27,070
Or customers are experimenting with money they will not keep spending.

71
00:04:27,380 --> 00:04:28,053
FRANK: Which is it?

72
00:04:28,403 --> 00:04:34,035
Nobody knows. That is why it costs $1.4 trillion to find out.

73
00:04:35,085 --> 00:04:40,596
Which brings us to the company everyone expected to go first. OpenAI.

74
00:04:40,646 --> 00:04:42,450
The maker of ChatGPT.

75
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:43,910
FRANK: That one I've heard of.

76
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:51,650
This month, OpenAI went to investors asking for a valuation of $1.4 trillion.

77
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:54,440
According to reports, investors pushed back.

78
00:04:54,490 --> 00:04:58,160
It settled for $1.25 trillion.

79
00:04:58,470 --> 00:05:01,352
FRANK: Asked for 1.4, got 1.25.

80
00:05:01,402 --> 00:05:04,284
I had that conversation with my wife about a boat.

81
00:05:04,634 --> 00:05:05,825
Why the pushback?

82
00:05:05,875 --> 00:05:16,166
The Wall Street Journal reported that last quarter, OpenAI took in $6.7 billion, and lost $12.3 billion running the business.

83
00:05:16,216 --> 00:05:19,889
For every dollar it earned, it lost almost two.

84
00:05:20,199 --> 00:05:21,923
FRANK: And that one's not on paper?

85
00:05:22,273 --> 00:05:24,243
That one is not on paper.

86
00:05:24,323 --> 00:05:28,558
OpenAI's own stock market debut has now been pushed to next year.

87
00:05:28,608 --> 00:05:35,343
So for the first time, the smaller rival may be valued higher than the company that started the boom.

88
00:05:35,653 --> 00:05:40,933
FRANK: So the one losing less money is worth more. Finally, something I understand.

89
00:05:41,283 --> 00:05:45,004
That is the story Wall Street is telling this week.

90
00:05:45,054 --> 00:05:48,022
Keep in mind who benefits from that story.

91
00:05:48,072 --> 00:05:51,793
A company about to sell $64 billion of stock.

92
00:05:52,103 --> 00:05:54,333
FRANK: Still waiting to hear about my fund.

93
00:05:54,683 --> 00:05:56,588
We're getting there, Frank.

94
00:05:57,638 --> 00:06:00,888
Now the part most headlines skip. Google.

95
00:06:01,408 --> 00:06:04,207
Google competes with both of these companies.

96
00:06:04,257 --> 00:06:07,463
Its own AI, Gemini, has a billion users.

97
00:06:07,513 --> 00:06:11,126
But Google also owns a large piece of Anthropic.

98
00:06:11,176 --> 00:06:14,382
Estimates range from about 10 to 14 percent.

99
00:06:14,432 --> 00:06:21,708
At the middle of the price range, that stake is worth around $130 billion.

100
00:06:22,018 --> 00:06:25,518
FRANK: Wait. Google owns part of the company it's competing with?

101
00:06:25,868 --> 00:06:30,819
And sells it the chips it runs on. Up to a million of them.

102
00:06:30,869 --> 00:06:35,463
Google's cloud business more than doubled last quarter, to over $30 billion.

103
00:06:35,743 --> 00:06:40,285
Think about that. If Anthropic wins, Google's shares go up.

104
00:06:40,335 --> 00:06:43,959
If Anthropic spends heavily, Google gets the money.

105
00:06:44,009 --> 00:06:47,633
And if Anthropic loses, Google still has Gemini.

106
00:06:47,943 --> 00:06:50,103
FRANK: So Google bet on every horse.

107
00:06:50,453 --> 00:06:51,948
And sells the hay.

108
00:06:52,308 --> 00:06:54,551
Amazon is in the same position.

109
00:06:54,601 --> 00:07:04,103
It is Anthropic's other giant shareholder, with a stake valued at around $140 billion, and it sells Anthropic computing power too.

110
00:07:04,153 --> 00:07:06,211
And they are not alone.

111
00:07:06,261 --> 00:07:14,642
Alongside the public sale, about $26 billion of stock is being sold directly to a small group of companies.

112
00:07:14,692 --> 00:07:18,858
Reports name Microsoft, Nvidia, Alphabet, and the chip maker Micron.

113
00:07:19,168 --> 00:07:22,153
FRANK: Hold on. Those are the people selling them the chips.

114
00:07:22,503 --> 00:07:25,225
They are buying shares in their own customer.

115
00:07:25,275 --> 00:07:29,382
If you watched our first video, you have seen this loop before.

116
00:07:29,432 --> 00:07:34,233
The money goes around in a circle, and everyone in the circle reports growth.

117
00:07:34,543 --> 00:07:39,583
FRANK: If I lent my best customer the money to shop at my store, my accountant would have questions.

118
00:07:39,933 --> 00:07:42,564
Those are the winners that were decided years ago.

119
00:07:42,614 --> 00:07:47,628
They bought in early, and they get paid on the way in, and on the way out.

120
00:07:48,678 --> 00:07:52,663
One more thing the filing tells you. Who is in charge.

121
00:07:52,823 --> 00:07:59,864
Last week we told you how Nvidia's founder could be removed, because he had no special shares.

122
00:07:59,914 --> 00:08:01,533
Anthropic is the opposite.

123
00:08:01,813 --> 00:08:05,422
Its seven founders own about 13 percent of the company.

124
00:08:05,472 --> 00:08:10,912
But through a special class of shares, they hold just over half of the votes.

125
00:08:10,962 --> 00:08:15,303
Amazon and Google, for all their money, have no vote at all.

126
00:08:15,613 --> 00:08:20,953
FRANK: $140 billion, and no vote? I get a vote at my bowling league.

127
00:08:21,263 --> 00:08:23,508
FRANK: And me? If my fund buys it?

128
00:08:23,858 --> 00:08:28,703
The people buying on Thursday, all together, get about 6 percent of the company.

129
00:08:29,013 --> 00:08:32,108
FRANK: So I'm paying for the bus, and I don't get to drive.

130
00:08:32,458 --> 00:08:38,206
You are a passenger. You may think founder control is a good thing or a bad thing.

131
00:08:38,256 --> 00:08:40,593
But know that it is the deal.

132
00:08:41,643 --> 00:08:48,163
Before you decide anything, look at what happened the last few times the whole country wanted the same stock.

133
00:08:48,323 --> 00:08:52,044
Facebook, 2012. The most anticipated debut of its era.

134
00:08:52,094 --> 00:08:54,307
It went public at $38.

135
00:08:54,357 --> 00:08:58,455
Within four months it had lost more than half its value.

136
00:08:58,505 --> 00:09:03,358
It took over a year just to get back to the starting price.

137
00:09:03,668 --> 00:09:08,548
FRANK: I remember. My neighbor bought it on day one. He stopped mentioning it by August.

138
00:09:08,898 --> 00:09:11,461
Uber, 2019. Priced at $45.

139
00:09:11,511 --> 00:09:18,928
It fell on its very first day, and spent most of the next year and a half below that price.

140
00:09:19,168 --> 00:09:22,166
Both companies went on to be worth far more.

141
00:09:22,216 --> 00:09:25,891
Facebook is now one of the most valuable companies on earth.

142
00:09:25,941 --> 00:09:29,278
So the lesson is not that these were bad businesses.

143
00:09:29,588 --> 00:09:30,983
FRANK: Then what is the lesson?

144
00:09:31,333 --> 00:09:35,117
The first day is usually the most expensive day to be excited.

145
00:09:35,167 --> 00:09:39,271
The people who did best were not the ones who bought the debut.

146
00:09:39,321 --> 00:09:45,023
They were the ones who owned it before, and the ones who waited until the excitement had passed.

147
00:09:45,333 --> 00:09:47,358
FRANK: My neighbor's mentioning it again now.

148
00:09:48,408 --> 00:09:51,103
So, Frank. What does this mean for you?

149
00:09:51,413 --> 00:09:53,298
FRANK: I've been asking that for ten minutes.

150
00:09:53,648 --> 00:09:59,332
It depends on which fund you own. Earlier this year, Nasdaq changed its rules.

151
00:09:59,382 --> 00:10:06,294
A giant new company can now join the Nasdaq-100 index after just 15 trading days.

152
00:10:06,344 --> 00:10:09,571
Before, the wait was at least three months.

153
00:10:09,621 --> 00:10:16,943
Reuters reports that Anthropic is likely to be added to several major indexes within 5 to 15 days.

154
00:10:16,993 --> 00:10:21,376
When that happens, every fund that tracks those indexes has to buy the stock.

155
00:10:21,426 --> 00:10:24,543
They do not get to wait for a better price.

156
00:10:24,853 --> 00:10:25,703
FRANK: Has to?

157
00:10:26,053 --> 00:10:26,754
Has to.

158
00:10:26,804 --> 00:10:35,760
When SpaceX went public in June, analysts estimated that index funds had to buy about $14 billion of its stock within three weeks.

159
00:10:35,810 --> 00:10:40,263
And only around 5 percent of the company was actually for sale.

160
00:10:40,573 --> 00:10:43,058
FRANK: Lot of buyers. Not a lot of stock.

161
00:10:43,408 --> 00:10:44,643
Now you're getting it.

162
00:10:44,923 --> 00:10:53,058
So if you own a Nasdaq-100 fund, or a total market fund, you will probably own a little Anthropic by the end of the year.

163
00:10:53,418 --> 00:10:58,458
But if you own an S&P 500 fund, you will not.

164
00:10:58,508 --> 00:11:00,640
That index kept its old rules.

165
00:11:00,690 --> 00:11:04,276
A company must be public for 12 months, and profitable.

166
00:11:04,326 --> 00:11:07,548
The earliest Anthropic could join is late next year.

167
00:11:07,858 --> 00:11:09,113
FRANK: Which one do I have?

168
00:11:09,463 --> 00:11:10,738
It's on your statement.

169
00:11:11,288 --> 00:11:12,953
FRANK: …I'm going to need my glasses.

170
00:11:13,303 --> 00:11:15,033
They're on your head, Frank.

171
00:11:15,083 --> 00:11:16,660
Same company. Same week.

172
00:11:16,710 --> 00:11:21,948
Two very different outcomes, depending on a line most people have never read.

173
00:11:22,998 --> 00:11:24,773
So here is what to watch.

174
00:11:25,133 --> 00:11:27,178
One. The price on Wednesday night.

175
00:11:27,228 --> 00:11:32,415
If it lands at the top of the range, or above it, demand is real.

176
00:11:32,465 --> 00:11:37,303
If it lands at the bottom, the $2 trillion whisper was the peak.

177
00:11:37,423 --> 00:11:39,940
Two. Where it closes on Thursday.

178
00:11:39,990 --> 00:11:45,928
Above $1.25 trillion, and Anthropic is officially worth more than OpenAI.

179
00:11:46,238 --> 00:11:48,943
FRANK: Thursday. Got it. I'll move my bowling night.

180
00:11:49,293 --> 00:11:50,383
Three. Mid December.

181
00:11:50,433 --> 00:11:56,083
That is when the index decision is due, and when the forced buying would begin.

182
00:11:56,393 --> 00:11:57,463
FRANK: And what do I do?

183
00:11:57,813 --> 00:11:59,343
That's not my call, Frank.

184
00:11:59,393 --> 00:12:03,768
But now you know what you'd own, who got paid first, and who's driving.

185
00:12:03,848 --> 00:12:08,400
Every boom has a moment when the public is finally invited in.

186
00:12:08,450 --> 00:12:12,618
Sometimes that moment is the beginning. Sometimes it is the top.

187
00:12:12,668 --> 00:12:18,133
The people who built this company, and the giants who funded it, have already been paid.

188
00:12:18,183 --> 00:12:21,593
On Thursday, it is your turn to decide what it is worth.

189
00:12:21,903 --> 00:12:23,188
FRANK: Nobody asked me.

190
00:12:23,538 --> 00:12:24,543
We just did.
